server, ntdll: list the pipes of the named pipe root directory

NtQueryDirectoryFile on the named pipe root directory (\\.\pipe\) failed with
STATUS_BAD_DEVICE_TYPE. The directory is a server object with no Unix fd, and
the client side of NtQueryDirectoryFile only reads Unix directories. Windows
lists the pipes there, and tools use that to find a running program by the
name of its pipe: FindFirstFile on \\.\pipe\*, Python's os.listdir and
.NET's Directory.GetFiles all failed, with ERROR_BAD_DEVICE_TYPE or "Bad
device type". The pbir CLI finds Power BI Desktop's local API through such a
listing (\\.\pipe\pbi-desktop-bridge-<PID>), so `pbir desktop` and `pbir
model -q` reported that no Desktop was running while it was.
The server now answers a private control code on the root directory with the
pipe names, their current instances and maximum instances, from a position it
keeps on the file object; the first query's search mask is kept there too,
as on Windows, so a later query without a mask goes on with it. ntdll
recognises the root directory by its object name, matches the mask with the
existing wildcard matcher and fills every directory information class: size
is the current instance count, allocation the maximum, attributes normal, as
Windows reports them. It then tells the server how many entries it used, so
an entry that did not fit is returned by the next query. The reply buffer is
virtual memory: the server checks it like a client buffer, and Unix heap
memory fails that check.
Checked with tests/e2e/pipe-directory-probe.c in a fresh prefix: 75 checks
(FindFirstFile with and without masks, every information class, the kept
mask, restart, a truncated entry, no match, no access, a closed pipe and
1,100 pipes read through FindNextFile); 14 passed and 27 failed before. With
Desktop open, `pbir desktop list`, `pbir desktop refresh` and `pbir desktop
screenshot` work, and so does `pbir model -q` once it can load an ADOMD.NET
client, which it needs on Windows too.
Wine-derived change: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c
